  "nameStory": "The Yellow Pinkgill gets its name from a fascinating contradiction - while 'luteum' means yellow in Latin, referring to its golden-yellow cap, it's called a 'pinkgill' because its spores are pink! When the mushroom matures, those pink spores dust the gills, creating a lovely rosy tint that contrasts beautifully with the sunny yellow cap above.",
  "description": "The Yellow Pinkgill is a small to medium-sized mushroom with a bright golden-yellow cap that catches your eye in the forest understory. It typically grows 1-3 inches across with a classic mushroom shape - a rounded cap sitting atop a slender stem. What makes this fungus special is the way its gills start pale but gradually develop a pink blush as the spores mature. You'll find them growing alone or in small groups directly from the soil in mixed woodlands.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Bright golden-yellow color, smooth to touch, rounded shape that may flatten with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Start whitish but develop a distinctive pink tint from maturing spores, attached to the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Pink colored - collect spores on white paper overnight to confirm this key identifying feature",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Slender, pale yellowish to whitish, feels firm and fibrous when gently squeezed",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Mild, pleasant mushroomy odor without any strong or unpleasant notes",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Safety",
      "description": "Never eat wild mushrooms without expert identification - many species can cause serious illness",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Yellow Pinkgills in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, where they grow directly from rich, moist soil. They prefer areas with good leaf litter and humus, often appearing along woodland paths or in clearings where dappled sunlight reaches the forest floor. You'll typically find them in the same areas where maples, oaks, and pines grow together.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely fruiting this early, though very late spring might produce a few specimens after warm, wet weather.",
    "summer": "Prime season begins in mid to late summer when warm temperatures combine with adequate rainfall to trigger fruiting.",
    "fall": "Peak fruiting time through early to mid-fall, especially after September rains. Look for fresh specimens with bright colors.",
    "winter": "No active fruiting - any remains from fall will be dark, decayed, and unidentifiable."
  },
  "funFact": "The spores of pinkgill mushrooms are actually angular rather than round like many mushrooms - they look like tiny geometric crystals under a microscope, almost like microscopic gemstones scattered through the forest!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most commonly confused with the Yellow Unicorn Entoloma (Entoloma murrayi), which has a more orange-tinted cap and a distinctive pointed bump (umbo) in the center. Remember: if the cap looks more orange than yellow or has a pointed 'unicorn horn' bump, it's probably the Yellow Unicorn, not the Yellow Pinkgill.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The genus name 'Entoloma' comes from Greek words meaning 'within' and 'fringe,' referring to the way the gills attach to the stem. Despite being called 'pinkgills,' many species in this group start with white or pale gills that only turn pink as they mature - it's the spore color, not the initial gill color, that gives them their common name.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's pinkgill species, Yellow Pinkgill stands out for its clean golden-yellow cap color - most other pinkgills are brown, gray, or other earth tones. The Yellow Unicorn Entoloma is its closest look-alike but has that telltale pointed bump in the cap center and more orange coloring. Unlike the Aborted Entoloma which can form weird blob-like shapes, Yellow Pinkgill always maintains a classic mushroom form.",
